   "readmeHtml": "<h1><code>Implementation/Compiler/</code></h1>\n<p>This folder compiles a <code>Gate</code> circuit to a <code>LowGate</code> program by sending each QFT, phase-product and modular-arithmetic block to its subroutine's lowerer, and proves that the compiled program acts exactly as the circuit does.</p>\n<p><code>Statement.lean</code> is the only file needed to see what is claimed: it holds every definition the claim depends on and imports nothing else from this folder. <code>Main.lean</code> proves the claim stated in <code>Statement.lean</code>; <code>Proofs/</code> holds the lemmas that proof uses, and the single-table forms used by <code>Emit/</code>, <code>GateCount/</code> and <code>Reference/</code>.</p>\n<h2>Background</h2>\n<p><code>Gate</code> has high-level blocks: the QFT, signed phase products (plain and controlled), and comparison and subtraction against a constant. <code>LowGate</code> has only elementary gates. The compiler walks the circuit, keeps sequencing and adjoints, copies elementary gates across, and replaces each high-level block by a lowered circuit: the QFT and phase products by those of <code>QFT/</code> and <code>PhaseProduct/</code>, and the constant comparison and subtraction by ones defined here.</p>\n<p><strong>Constant arithmetic.</strong> Both use one spare qubit of a scratch register as the constant 1, copy the bits of N into the scratch register with one CNOT per set bit, and negate it to get \u2212N. The comparison then adds the data register to the scratch register, copies the sign of data \u2212 N to the flag, and uncomputes. The subtraction controls the preparation of \u2212N on the flag, adds the scratch register to the data register, and uncomputes.</p>\n<p>Those lowered circuits borrow spare qubits as scratch space. Each block therefore needs two things:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Enough spare qubits, laid out correctly.</strong> This is a static condition on</li></ol>\n<p>the registers, needed to build the lowered circuit at all.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Those spare qubits in |0\u27e9 when the block runs.</strong> This is a condition on</li></ol>\n<p>the state, needed for the lowered circuit to act like the block.</p>\n<p>Which Toom-Cook table the phase products use is chosen by a <code>ShorLoweringPolicy</code>, a list of tables indexed by operand width. A single table is the constant policy.</p>\n<h2><code>Statement.lean</code></h2>\n<p>The following explains each section of the code in the Statement.lean file</p>\n<p><strong>Constant arithmetic.</strong> <code>lowerCmpGeConst</code> and <code>lowerCSubConst</code>, the lowered comparison and subtraction described above; <code>ConstArithmeticWorkspace</code>, the spare qubits and layout they need; and <code>CmpGeConstCleanState</code> / <code>CSubConstCleanState</code>, the condition that those spare qubits start at zero.</p>\n<p><strong>Static workspace condition.</strong> <code>Policy.GateWorkspaceOK P G</code>: every high-level block in <code>G</code> has the spare qubits its lowerer needs under the policy <code>P</code>. The ideal modular multiplication has no lowering, so it never satisfies it.</p>\n<p><strong>The lowering.</strong> <code>Policy.lowerGate P G hws</code>: the compiled program. It takes a proof <code>hws</code> of the static condition, since the subroutine lowerers need it to build their circuits.</p>\n<p><strong>Clean-state precondition.</strong> <code>Policy.GateWorkspaceCleanState</code>: the spare qubits each block will use are in |0\u27e9 on the state it is applied to. For a sequence, the second part's condition is checked on the state after the first part's compiled program.</p>\n<p><strong>Correctness.</strong> <code>Policy.LowerGateCorrect</code>: for an admissible policy, on any state meeting the clean-state precondition, <code>\u27e6Policy.lowerGate P G hws\u27e7\u2097 \u03c8 = \u27e6G\u27e7 \u03c8</code>.</p>\n<h2><code>Main.lean</code></h2>\n<p>Proves <code>Policy.LowerGateCorrect</code>.</p>"
